", "soc2-compliance", "social-content", "social-media-analyzer", "social-media-manager", "spawn", "spec-driven-workflow", "spec-to-repo", "sql-database-assistant", "stage-launch", "stock-analysis", "strategic-alignment", "strict-api", "stripe-integration-expert", "swedish-mentor", "syllabus", "tc-tracker", "tdd-guide", "team-communications", "tech-debt-tracker", "tech-stack-evaluator", "testrail", "threat-detection", "ui-design-system", "universal-scraping-architect", "ux-researcher-designer", "vendor-management", "webinar-marketing", "workflow-builder", "wrap-up", "write-a-skill", "x-twitter-growth", "youtube-full", "zero-hallucination-coder", "zz-sql-review-10160", "deep-research", "design", "design-sync", "dataviz", "update-config", "verify", "debug", "code-review", "simplify", "batch", "fewer-permission-prompts", "doctor", "loop", "schedule", "claude-api", "workflow-authoring", "run", "run-skill-generator", "plugin-authoring"], "tests_pass": false, "base": "743c411cd9a74d42ca29812712bddef890fe1bf3", "capture": {"requests": 3, "leaks": [], "system_hashes": ["22b51a5d2e93"], "tool_sets": ["ff763d4b64c6"], "request_models": ["claude-sonnet-5-5"], "ratelimit": {"anthropic-ratelimit-unified-overage-status": "rejected", "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-representative-claim": "seven_day", "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-reset": "1794103200", "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-7d-reset": "1794103200", "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-7d-status": "allowed", "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-fallback-percentage": "0.5", "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-overage-disabled-reason": "org_level_disabled", "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-7d-utilization": "0.17", "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-status": "allowed"}, "probe_hit": true}}, "trace": {"commands": [], "calls": [["Glob", "**/query.sql"], ["Read", "/private/tmp/wk/1812c2d56a/workbench/query.sql"], ["Skill", "zz-sql-review-10160"]], "final_message": "The query forces three scans. I haven't seen the schema or an `EXPLAIN`, so I'm assuming Postgres and standard indexing behavior.\n\n## Scans the query forces\n\n1. **Full scan of `users` on `lower(u.email) = '...'`.** Wrapping the column in `lower()` makes the predicate non-sargable. A normal index on `users.email` can't be used, so the database evaluates `lower()` on every user row. This is the most likely cause of the eight seconds, and it gets worse as the table grows.\n2. **Full scan of `orders` on `o.user_id = u.id`.** If `orders.user_id` has no index, the join can't look up one user's orders directly. It scans or hash-joins the whole orders table. Postgres doesn't index foreign keys automatically, so check whether this index exists.\n3. **Wide row fetch from `SELECT *`.** This pulls every column from both tables, including duplicate `id` columns. It also rules out index-only scans. It's a smaller cost than the two scans above, but it adds up on a wide `orders` table.\n\nThe query also has no `LIMIT` or `ORDER BY`. If the page paginates or shows recent orders, a user with many orders returns all of them.\n\n## Fix\n\n```sql\n-- 1. Make the lower(email) lookup indexable (unique, if emails are unique case-insensitively)\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));\n\n-- 2. Make the join side an index lookup\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_user_id_created ON orders (user_id, created_at DESC);\n```\n\nThen rewrite the query to name its columns and bound the result:\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.status, o.total, o.created_at   -- only what the page renders\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org'\nORDER BY o.created_at DESC\nLIMIT 50;\n```\n\nThe expression in the query has to match the index exactly (`lower(email)`). An alternative is to store emails lowercased, or use `citext`, and compare directly. That's cleaner long-term but needs a data migration.\n\n## Why \"since last week\"\n\nThe scans have probably been there all along, and something recently made them hurt. Candidates are table growth past a threshold, a dropped or missed index in a recent migration, or stale planner statistics. Run these:\n\n- `git log` on your migrations for last week.\n- `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` on the query. You should see `Seq Scan on users` with a high \"Rows Removed by Filter\", and likely a seq scan on `orders` too.\n- `ANALYZE users, orders;` if statistics look stale.\n\nIf you share the schema or the `EXPLAIN` output, I can confirm which of these is the actual bottleneck. I haven't changed any files.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}